course: dictionary
lesson: 5675
title: One word, many meanings
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Many words have more than one meaning, and the context tells you which one is right. Load is a good example. «Helping with the washing up or loading the dishwasher is useful work at home.» Here loading is a doing word: it means putting things into a machine.
explain: «With several pulleys, more lengths of rope share the load, so you pull with less force but must pull much more rope.» Here load is a naming word: it means the weight that is carried or lifted. Ask yourself: is something being done, or is something being carried?
explain: «Loads of friends came to the party.» Loads does not mean heavy weight here. It means a lot. Try the swap test: put "a lot of" into the sentence. If it still makes sense, you have the right meaning.
explain: «A brick short of a load» (Australia) means stupid or a bit mad, not a missing pile of bricks. When a phrase sounds silly word by word, look at the whole sentence and its clues.
fact: Load can be a naming word (a load, loads) or a doing word (load, loads, loaded, loading).
fact: A load is something heavy that is carried, for example by a truck, a person or a washing machine.
fact: To load means to put things into or onto a car, ship, machine or bag.
fact: Loads of means a lot, and it is an informal way of speaking.
fact: To overload means to put too much on something, which can be unsafe, as with a bag, a plug or a boat.
exercise: «Helping with the washing up or loading the dishwasher is useful work at home.» In this sentence, what does loading mean? | Making a computer page open | Carrying something very heavy up a hill | A large crowd of people | *Putting things into a machine
exercise: «Loads of friends came to my party.» What does loads mean here? | A heavy weight | Glowing brightly | *A lot | Putting things onto a ship
exercise: «A loaded ship sits lower in the water.» What does loaded mean here? | Floating very high | Opened on a computer | Glowing in the dark | *Carrying a heavy cargo
test: «More lengths of rope share the load, so you pull with less force.» What does load mean here? | To put things into a bag | A lot of friends | Glowing softly | *The weight that is lifted
test: Nadia says, «He's a brick short of a load.» What does it mean? | A pile of bricks is missing | *Stupid or a bit mad | Very tall and strong | A heavy weight of bricks
exam: Jayden asks, «Is load a naming word or a doing word?» What is the best answer? | Only a doing word, never a naming word | *Both: a load is a naming word, and to load is a doing word | Neither, it is a describing word | Only a naming word, never a doing word
